 more than half of syria's people  already hungry the agricultural crisis  couldn't come at a worse time zen  al jazeera  all right well for more on this let's  speak to marco sanchez the deputy  director of the agri-food economics  division at food and agricultural  organization he's joining us live from  skype via brussels thanks for your time  sir as we heard there this seems like a  perfect storm of problems you've got the  war in ukraine affecting supply chains  you've got drought in certain parts of  the world how is the agricultural sector  surviving through this  um thank you very much my pleasure to be  here well you know  the way i call this is just the tip of  the iceberg what is happening today in  agri-food systems uh  is very worrisome  uh but basically is the confirmation of  something that the food and culture  organization of the united nations have  been observing for many many years  uh back in 2017 when we launched the  same report that we are launching today  but back then we had identified conflict  as a very key driver of foreign security  and managed issue in the world we will  observe the hangar was declining for a  decade and then all of a sudden it  started to increase again and we're  wondering what is going on so we we  started identifying these key drivers  and conflict turned out to be one of  them today we have a conflict but to us  it is not new it's just a conflict that  engages two big players in many respects  for like the good systems but it's a  conflict that we knew already conflicts  all over the world were affecting the  numbers but also the other key drivers  are  climate climate adversities climate  adversity that we can no longer predict  uncertainty that's another of the key  drivers of food in security and  malnutrition and then you add to that  economic downturns that we're observing  even before the covet 19 pandemic there  were crisis economic crisis all over the  world that were increasing the problems  now the covenanting pandemic comes just  to add with all its strength  more economic slowed down due to the  measures to contain it  and these key drivers climate conflict  and economic slowdowns are creating more  problems because not only are they  intensifying themselves but also they  are having combined effects  and on top of that they create a lot of  problems for the more vulnerable because  of the existing inequalities the word  comes and makes things worse as you say  but it's just the tip of the eyebrow you  know  as you mentioned there of course how do  you even measure hunger it obviously  means different things to different  people in the same way that poverty  means different things to different  people around the world how would you  define hunger  sure we have uh the the the let's say  the traditional indicator that we use  which is one of the official united  nations indicators to trace uh  the evolution or the progress of the  world toward the zero hunger goal which  is a sustainable development goal is  called the prevalence of the nourishment  and basically what this number tries to  do is through official data that feo  receives from countries i understand if  people are at least you know meeting the  minimum energy requirements to live a  healthy life  now hunger is chronic and that's why we  measure it to the prevalence of the  nourishment but  the food and security problem is bigger  than hunger  and what we have been doing also is  creating  a  new indicators precisely because of what  you said  the people may have different ways of  both defining hunger but also  experiencing hunger so fao in this  report in 19 and 2019  released a new indicator that we call  the moderate or severe food insecurity  and this indicator is differently  constructed because it is based on  surveys that we ask to households to  peoples to children to women to the the  head of the household to understand what  is the perception what is their  experience in terms of the hunger 